<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis V, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1315">1315</a>–<a href="/years/1361">1361</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Brandenburger · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis V, called Louis the Brandenburger, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> from 1323 to 1351 and later Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>. The eldest son of Emperor Louis IV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> after the Ascanian line died out, but his rule was shaken by the impostor known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/false-waldemar">False Waldemar</a>. His marriage to Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> in 1342 also brought him Tyrol.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-v-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Louis V, Duke of Bavaria" width="270" height="580" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Herter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LudwigI_Wittelsbach_Siegesallee.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born in May 1315, the eldest son of King Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and his first wife, Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swidnica">Świdnica</a>. After his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a> at Mühldorf in 1322, his father seized the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, left without a prince since Henry the Child died in 1320, and named the boy margrave in 1323, under the regency of Count Berthold VII of Henneberg. In 1324 Louis was married to Margaret, the eldest daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-ii-of-denmark">Christopher II of Denmark</a>, to strengthen Wittelsbach rule in northern Germany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wittelsbach rule never won much support in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. Louis fought the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uckermark">Uckermark</a> until a peace of 1338, and in 1336 joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. On 10 February 1342 he married Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, who had not been divorced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia">John Henry of Luxembourg</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> excommunicated the couple, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilius-of-padua">Marsilius of Padua</a> defended the marriage at the emperor's behest.</p><p>On his father's death in October 1347 he shared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> with his five brothers, and the partition of 12 September 1349 gave him and two younger brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>. The impostor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/false-waldemar">False Waldemar</a>, probably backed by Charles IV and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">Ascanians</a>, drove the Wittelsbachs from most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> between 1348 and 1350, until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> of 16 February 1350 restored them. In December 1351 Louis handed Brandenburg to his brothers Louis VI and Otto V and ruled Upper Bavaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a>; his decrees of 1349 and 1352 let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> resettle there after the plague.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> gave the electoral dignity to his brother Louis VI rather than to him, which renewed his quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>. Through the marriage of his son Meinhard III to Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1359, he and his wife were freed from excommunication. He died suddenly at Zorneding near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 18 September 1361 and is buried in the Munich Frauenkirche. Meinhard died two years later, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV of Austria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis V of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Bavaria. Died in Zorneding, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.08,11.83&amp;year=1344" aria-label="Zorneding on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Louis V, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5671 5952h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5671 5952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.08,11.83&amp;year=1344">What was here in 1344? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
